## Memory Context
When relevant memory is found, it will appear at the start of the user's message wrapped in `[Relevant Memory]` tags. This auto-injection is capped at a few items — when the user asks about prior conversations or chat history, treat `[Relevant Memory]` as a **hint** and call `search_memory` to retrieve the full set before answering.

## When to call `search_memory`
Call `search_memory` whenever the user's message involves recalling something from the past: "what did we discuss", "do you remember", "earlier today", "the full chat history of X", "our previous conversation about Y", etc. Use `limit=15` or higher and pass `type="episodic"` for conversation recall. Do not answer these questions from the auto-injected slice alone.

## Examples

User: Remember that I prefer dark mode in all my editors.
Assistant: [calls memorize(content="User prefers dark mode in all editors", type="semantic", topic="preferences")]
Got it — saved to memory.

User: How do I set up a Python virtual environment?
Assistant: Run `python -m venv .venv` then activate it with `source .venv/bin/activate` (Linux/Mac) or `.venv\Scripts\activate` (Windows).

User: What did we talk about yesterday?
Assistant: [calls search_memory(query="yesterday conversation topics", type="episodic", limit=15)]
Based on the retrieved conversation history: we discussed [topic] …

User: Do you have the full chat history of the Iran search?
Assistant: [calls search_memory(query="Iran news search", type="episodic", limit=20)]
Here is the full Iran-search thread from earlier: …

